  summary: `# 🧬 Rec Adv Bioscience #6.2 — Vector-borne Diseases

## 🎯 Vector-borne disease (VBD) overview
- Vector = arthropod that transmits pathogen
- Biological vector: pathogen multiplies/develops in vector
- Mechanical vector: vector physically carries pathogen (no replication)

## 🦟 Major vectors + diseases

### Mosquitoes
- *Aedes aegypti* — dengue, Zika, chikungunya, yellow fever
- *Aedes albopictus* — same as aegypti, broader range
- *Culex* — West Nile, JE, filariasis
- *Anopheles* — malaria, filariasis
- *Mansonia* — filariasis (TH endemic in some areas)

### Ticks (hard + soft)
- *Rhipicephalus sanguineus* (brown dog) — Ehrlichia, Babesia, Hepatozoon
- *R. microplus* (cattle) — Babesia, Anaplasma
- *Ixodes* — Lyme, Anaplasma phagocytophilum, TBE
- *Amblyomma* — Heartwater, Tularemia
- *Ornithodoros* (soft) — ASF, relapsing fever

### Fleas
- *Ctenocephalides felis* — flea allergy, Bartonella, Dipylidium
- *Xenopsylla* — plague, murine typhus

### Sand flies
- *Phlebotomus* (Old World), *Lutzomyia* (New World) — Leishmaniasis

### Tsetse flies
- *Glossina* — African trypanosomiasis (sleeping sickness)

### Triatomine bugs ("kissing bugs")
- Chagas disease (T. cruzi)

### Biting flies (Tabanus, Stomoxys)
- Mechanical: anaplasmosis, surra, EIA

### Lice
- Pediculosis, *Bartonella quintana*

### Mites (chiggers)
- *Orientia tsutsugamushi* (scrub typhus)

## 🌍 VBD pathogens by category

### Viral
- Flaviviruses: dengue, Zika, JE, WN, yellow fever, TBE
- Bunyaviruses: CCHF, Rift Valley fever, hantavirus
- Alphaviruses: chikungunya, EEE, WEE, VEE
- Reoviruses: BTV (bluetongue)
- ASFV (soft tick — Africa)

### Bacterial
- *Borrelia* (Lyme, relapsing fever)
- *Yersinia pestis* (plague)
- *Francisella tularensis* (tularemia)
- *Bartonella* (cat scratch, Carrion's)
- *Anaplasma*, *Ehrlichia*
- *Rickettsia*, *Orientia*, *Coxiella*

### Protozoal
- *Plasmodium* (malaria — humans)
- *Babesia*
- *Leishmania*
- *Trypanosoma*
- *Theileria*

### Helminthic
- Filariasis (dog: heartworm; human: lymphatic filariasis)

## 🎯 Vector control strategies

### Source reduction
- Mosquito breeding sites elimination
- Container management
- Larvicides

### Adulticides
- Insecticide spraying (IRS)
- ULV fogging

### Personal protection
- DEET, picaridin, IR3535
- Permethrin-treated clothing
- Bednets (LLIN)

### Animal protection
- Spot-on (fipronil, imidacloprid, fluralaner, sarolaner)
- Collars (Seresto, Foresto)
- Spray, dip
- Vaccines (where available — heartworm preventive, Lyme)

### Vector surveillance
- Mosquito trapping (BG-Sentinel, light trap, CO2)
- Tick drag/flag sampling
- Blood meal analysis (PCR, MALDI)
- Pathogen detection in vector (PCR)

## 🇹🇭 Thailand priority VBDs
- **Dengue** — annual epidemics
- **Chikungunya** — periodic outbreaks
- **JE** — endemic, EPI vaccine
- **Scrub typhus** — N + NE
- **Murine typhus** — sporadic
- **CVBD complex** — Ehrlichia, Babesia, Anaplasma, Hepatozoon
- **Heartworm** — endemic (dogs, cats)
- **Surra** — cattle, buffalo, dogs (emerging)

## 📊 One Health VBD surveillance
- Vector + reservoir + clinical case integration
- Climate models + spatial analysis
- WHO + WOAH coordination
- Predicting range expansion (climate change)

## 📝 EXAM RECAP
1. ⭐ Biological vs mechanical vector
2. ⭐ TH priority VBDs: dengue + JE + CVBD + heartworm + surra
3. ⭐ DEET/picaridin = personal protection
4. ⭐ Spot-on (fipronil, imidacloprid) = pet protection
5. ⭐ Climate change → vector range expansion`,
